# Field chapter 8 · Conversation. The book so far tells and proves. Here people talk: a play in five scenes,
# with many speakers, quick turns, and the small words the other books do not need (hello, please, sorry, oh,
# well, maybe, really). Read it to hear how talk sounds, not only how it is built.
## conversation

(talk/VERB (with (friend many)) (SUBJ (person every)))                       # Every person talks with friends.
(talk/VERB always (SUBJ we))                                                 # We always talk.
(say (OBJ (word small)) (SUBJ we))                                           # We say small words:
@verse
(and hello/INTJ goodbye/NOUN)                                                # hello and goodbye,
(and please/INTJ thanks/NOUN sorry/INTJ)                                     # please, thanks and sorry,
(and yes/INTJ nah/INTJ okay/INTJ)                                            # yes, nah and okay,
(and oh/INTJ ah/INTJ wow/INTJ huh/INTJ)                                      # oh, ah, wow and huh,
(and well/ADV maybe/ADV really/ADV)                                          # well, maybe and really.
@prose
(help (OBJ (talk/VERB (SUBJ we))) (SUBJ (word this)))                        # These words help us talk.
(ask (OBJ (whether (talk/VERB (SUBJ (person other/DET)))))                   # We ask whether the other person talks,
  (SUBJ we))
(hear (OBJ (person other/DET the)) (SUBJ we))                                # and we hear the other person.

## (play/NOUN (of (market the)))
(be (play/NOUN a (in (scene five))) (SUBJ this))                             # This is a play in five scenes.
@verse
(person (of (play/NOUN the)))                                                # The persons of the play:
("Sol" (baker a))                                                            # Sol, a baker.
("Nima" (with (apple many)))                                                 # Nima, with many apples.
("Kofi" (with (fish many)))                                                  # Kofi, with fish.
("Tomas" (man/NOUN old a))                                                   # Tomas, an old man.
("Ada" (girl small a))                                                       # Ada, a small girl.
("Lena" (mother (of "Ada") the))                                             # Lena, the mother of Ada.
("Yuki" (stranger a))                                                        # Yuki, a stranger.
("Omar" (boy a (with (dog a))))                                              # Omar, a boy with a dog.
("Pip" (dog small the))                                                      # Pip, the small dog.
@prose
